>>
Rule 1 (dimension compatibility of input tensors for tensorsolve)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= ndim(v_1) ≥ ndim(v_2)
>>
Rule 2 (axes length constraint based on the dimensions of input tensors)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor, v_3 : tuple(int)} |= v_3.len = ndim(v_1) - ndim(v_2)
>>
Rule 3 (axis indices in axes parameter must be within the valid range of input tensor dimensions)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tuple(int)} |= ∀i ∈ [0, v_2.len - 1] : (v_2[i] ≥ 0 - ndim(v_1)) ∧ (v_2[i] < ndim(v_1))
>>
Rule 4 (default axis alignment when axes is not specified)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= ndim(v_1) ≥ ndim(v_2) ∧ (if ndim(v_2) > 0 then (∀i ∈ [0, ndim(v_2) - 1] : shape(v_1, i) = shape(v_2, i)))
>>
Rule 5 (input tensors must have floating-point or complex data types)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= dtype_(v_1) ≥ 6 ∧ dtype_(v_1) ≤ 10 ∧ dtype_(v_2) ≥ 6 ∧ dtype_(v_2) ≤ 10
>>
Rule 6 (dimension compatibility of the two input tensors)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= ndim(v_1) ≥ ndim(v_2) ∧ ndim(v_2) ≥ 1
>>
Rule 9 (input tensors must have matching data types)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= dtype_(v_1) = dtype_(v_2)
>>
Rule 11 (the number of dimensions of tensor a is twice that of tensor b when the system is partitioned symmetrically)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= ndim(v_1) = 2 * ndim(v_2)
